course: biology
lesson: 354
title: Skin: fingerprints, scabs, scars, bruises and blisters
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Press an inky finger onto paper and you get a pattern of ridges that nobody else in the world has, not even an identical twin.
explain: Skin is also a brilliant repair kit. When you graze your knee, blood dries into a scab that seals the gap like a plaster while new skin grows underneath.
explain: A deep wound mends with tough fibres instead of ordinary skin, so it leaves a scar; a knock that breaks tiny blood vessels without breaking the skin leaves a bruise that changes colour as the body cleans it up.
explain: Skin even fights back against rubbing: a blister cushions a sore spot, and a guitarist's fingertips grow thick hard calluses.
fact: Fingerprints are unique; even identical twins have different ones, and they stay the same all your life, only bigger, which is why the police use them.
fact: The ridges form before birth, shaped by how the baby's skin grows and moves in the womb.
fact: Fingerprint patterns are loops, whorls and arches; most people have mostly loops, then whorls, and arches are the rarest, and the patterns are partly inherited.
fact: Koalas have fingerprints so like ours that they could confuse a detective.
fact: The ridges help you grip and help you feel textures, because stroking a surface makes tiny vibrations that nerves pick up; a very few people are born with no fingerprints.
fact: A scab is dried blood and tiny threads that seal a wound while new skin grows underneath; picking it can let germs in and slow healing, and healing skin often itches.
fact: A scar forms when a deep wound heals with tough fibres; scar tissue has no hair or sweat glands, and scars fade over years but rarely vanish completely.
fact: A bruise is blood leaked under the skin from broken tiny vessels; it turns from red-purple to blue, then green, then yellow as the body breaks the blood down, and fades in a week or two.
fact: A blister is a bubble of fluid between skin layers that forms from rubbing and cushions the damaged skin; it is best not popped, so it heals under its own skin.
fact: Wounds inside the mouth heal faster than wounds on the skin, helped by saliva and a good blood supply.
fact: The body slowly pushes out small things like splinters, but a splinter can get sore and red, so a grown-up should take it out with clean tweezers, and a deep one may need a doctor.
fact: Rubbing makes skin grow thicker into calluses, which protect it; barefoot runners grow thick soles that protect like shoes.
exercise: Do identical twins have the same fingerprints? | No, their fingerprints are different | Yes, exactly the same on every finger | Yes, but only the prints on their two thumbs | No, twins have no fingerprints at all
exercise: When do fingerprint ridges form? | Before birth | At about age five | When you start writing | Only when you are a grown-up
exercise: Which fingerprint pattern is the rarest? | Arches | Loops | Whorls | They are all equally common
exercise: Which animal has fingerprints very like ours? | The koala | The pet dog | The racehorse | The parrot
exercise: How do fingertip ridges help you? | They help you grip and feel textures | They let your fingers sweat a lot less | They hold your fingernails firmly in place | They make your fingers grow a bit longer
exercise: What is a scab? | Dried blood and threads sealing a wound | A thick layer of dirt that has dried over a cut | Dead skin that never comes off | A kind of germ on the skin
exercise: Why should you not pick a scab? | It can let germs in and slow healing | It will turn into a bruise | It makes the skin grow a fingerprint | Scabs are poisonous to touch
exercise: What forms a scar? | A deep wound healing with tough fibres | A bruise that never faded away and stayed for good | Too much sun on one spot | A scab that was left on far too long
exercise: What has scar tissue lost? | Hair and sweat glands | All of its feeling for ever | Its blood supply for ever | Its skin colour for ever
exercise: What is a bruise? | Blood leaked under the skin from broken tiny vessels | A burn hidden under the skin where you cannot see the heat | A bubble of fluid between layers | Dirt trapped under the skin
exercise: Which colour comes last as a bruise heals? | Yellow | Red-purple | Blue | Black
exercise: What is a blister? | A bubble of fluid between skin layers | Blood pooled under the skin | A small burn scar | An infected scab full of germs
exercise: What usually makes a blister on your heel? | Rubbing from shoes | Too much sweating | Eating too much salt | Cold weather
exercise: Which heals faster, a bite inside the cheek or a cut on the arm of the same size? | The bite inside the cheek | The cut on the arm | They always heal at the same speed | Neither ever heals
exercise: What is the safest way to deal with a sore splinter? | A grown-up removes it with clean tweezers | Leave it in for years until it dissolves | Push it in deeper so it vanishes | Cut around it with sharp scissors
exercise: What are calluses? | Thick hard skin grown from rubbing | Scars left behind by old burns long ago | Old blisters that filled up with blood | Old bruises that have slowly gone hard
test: «my twin's fingerprints are the same as mine, we can unlock each other's phones» What is right? | Even identical twins have different fingerprints | Yes, twins share all their fingerprints, every single line | Only the thumbs match in twins | Twins share fingerprints until they are ten
test: Count them: a boy finds 6 loops, 3 whorls and some arches on his ten fingers. How many arches? | 1 | 0 | 3 | 2
test: «leave my scab alone, I'm picking it, it's mine» What is the best advice? | Leave it, because picking can let germs in and slow healing | Pick it, because it lets the air in and the skin heals faster that way | Pick it, scabs are full of germs | Put salt on it to dry it
test: A bruise on Monday is red-purple. What colour is it most likely to be on Friday or Saturday? | Green or yellow | Bright red | Black | White
test: In a Tom and Jerry cartoon Tom is hit on the head and a huge bump shoots up in a second like a balloon, then vanishes. What really happens? | A bump swells more slowly and can take days to go down | Bumps can grow even taller than the head and then pop | Bumps disappear in seconds | A knock never causes swelling
test: «new shoes gave me a blister, I'm popping it with a pin» What is the best advice? | Do not pop it; let it heal under its own skin | Pop it and peel off the loose skin so it dries faster | Pop it with any pin, it does not matter | Rub it until it goes away
test: Why does a scar feel different from the skin around it? | It is tough fibres with no hair or sweat glands | It is made of a thin plate of bone growing in the skin | It is a scab that never fell off | It is dead skin with no blood at all
test: «bit my cheek at lunch, it'll take a month to heal» What is right? | Mouth wounds usually heal fast, in days | It will never heal at all | It needs a plaster to heal | It heals slower than skin
test: Why are fingerprints useful to the police? | They are unique and stay the same all your life | They change a little every year, so they show your age | Everyone in a family shares them | They show what you ate
test: What would be harder for someone born without fingerprints? | Gripping and feeling fine textures | Seeing in the dark | Hearing music | Tasting sweets
test: A guitarist practises every day for months. What happens to her fingertips? | They grow thick protective calluses | They lose their fingerprints for ever | They get thinner and softer each year | They grow extra fingernails on their palms
test: «why's my scab itchy, is it infected» What is the most likely reason? | Itching is often normal while new skin heals | Itching always means a bad infection that needs a doctor | The scab is growing hair | The scab is turning into a bruise
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the fingerprints of identical twins. | The fingerprints of such twins differ one from the other | The fingerprints of such twins are identical in every respect | The fingerprints of such twins coincide upon the thumbs alone | Such twins possess no fingerprints
exam: Identify the principal function of a scab. | The sealing of a wound whilst new skin forms beneath it | The permanent replacement of damaged skin | The importation of germs into the wound | The darkening of the skin about the wound
exam: Which of the following sequences most accurately characterises the change in colour of a healing bruise? | Red-purple, then blue, then green, then yellow | Yellow, then green, then blue, then red | Red, then black, then white | Blue throughout, until it vanishes
exam: State the recommended treatment of a blister occasioned by friction. | It is to be left unpopped, that it may heal beneath its own skin | It is to be popped forthwith with a pin | It is to be rubbed until it disperses | It is to be treated with salt
